  {
    "number": 547,
    "title": "README and install.sh call the Linux release a \"static binary\"; release.yml documents a glibc 2.35 floor",
    "author": "cargopete",
    "state": "CLOSED",
    "url": "https://github.com/nightswatchhq/nuthatch/issues/547",
    "body": "Found while verifying the claims in PR #541 before merging it. **Filed, not worked** - the sprint\nbrief says work discovered mid-sprint is filed for the board.\n\n## The claim\n\n`README.md` (Install section, as merged in #541) and the served `install.sh` both say the same\nthing:\n\n> That downloads the prebuilt **static** binary for your platform from the latest release …\n\n`install.sh`, line 4: `# Downloads the prebuilt static binary for your platform from the latest\nGitHub release`.\n\n## What the release actually builds\n\n`release.yml` builds `x86_64-unknown-linux-gnu` with a plain `cargo build --release --locked\n--target …`. No musl target, no `-C target-feature=+crt-static`. The Linux binary is dynamically\nlinked against glibc, and the workflow knows it - the comment above the matrix entry is explicit:\n\n> A dynamically-linked binary requires the glibc it was built against *or newer*, so the builder's\n> glibc is the floor for every system that can run the release. […] It surfaced when the container\n> job's smoke test ran the binary against `debian:bookworm-slim` (glibc 2.36) and got\n> `version 'GLIBC_2.39' not found`.\n\nSo the workflow pins `ubuntu-22.04` for a glibc 2.35 floor, and one file away the install\ninstructions tell the reader no such floor exists.\n\n## Why this is not simply a wrong word\n\n\"Static binary\" is house vocabulary here, and it is `CLAUDE.md` non-negotiable 1, where it means\n*self-contained, zero external services* - no Postgres, no Docker, no IPFS, DuckDB extensions linked\nin rather than loaded (`tests/duckdb_extensions_are_static.rs`). In that sense it is true, and it is\nused that way in eight or so places across the README, `docs/operators.md` and the RFCs. **Those are\nnot the problem and should not be touched.**\n\nThe install section is the one place where the reader is not being told about the architecture, they\nare being told what will happen when they run a command on their machine - and in that context\n\"static\" reads as \"will run on any Linux\", which is the exact thing the glibc floor denies.\n\n## Suggested repair, if the board wants it\n\nOne word in one place: in the README Install section and in `install.sh`'s header comment, say\n*prebuilt binary* and, if it is worth the line, name the floor - \"Linux builds target glibc 2.35 and\nnewer (Debian 12, Ubuntu 22.04, RHEL 9 and later)\". Leave every architectural use of \"static binary\"\nalone; `CLAUDE.md`'s decisions are not ours to edit.\n\nNote `install.sh` is served from the website and lives in the frontend repo, so that half needs its\nown change and its own deploy - PR #541 already says so about a different `install.sh` defect (its\nthree build-from-source fallbacks still recommend the unpinned `cargo install --git`, which is #534\nall over again for exactly the users who cannot take a binary).\n"
  },
  {
    "number": 946,
    "title": "The release binary requires GLIBCXX_3.4.29 and the README documents only a glibc floor",
    "author": "cargopete",
    "state": "CLOSED",
    "url": "https://github.com/nightswatchhq/nuthatch/issues/946",
    "body": "Found by RFC-0042 slice 0's BOM (#935), measured on the **published** artefact rather than a local\nbuild - a local one carries the build host's floors, and I nearly reported `GLIBC_2.39` from mine\nbefore catching it.\n\n```\ndynamic deps: libstdc++.so.6, libgcc_s.so.1, libm.so.6, libc.so.6\nhighest GLIBC:   GLIBC_2.34\nhighest GLIBCXX: GLIBCXX_3.4.29\nhighest CXXABI:  CXXABI_1.3.13\n```\n\n## The gap\n\nREADME says: *\"dynamically linked; needs glibc 2.35 or newer - Debian 12, Ubuntu 22.04, RHEL 9 and\nAmazon Linux 2023 all clear that floor\"*. It says nothing about libstdc++, and the binary links it\nbecause DuckDB is C++.\n\n`GLIBCXX_3.4.29` means libstdc++ from **GCC 11 or newer**. Every platform the README names clears it\n(Debian 12 ships GCC 12, Ubuntu 22.04 GCC 11/12, RHEL 9 GCC 11 - exactly at the floor). So this is\n**incompleteness, not a broken promise**: a reader on a system with new glibc and old libstdc++ is\nsurprised by a requirement we never stated.\n\n## Also worth recording, and worth leaving alone\n\nThe measured GLIBC floor is **2.34**; the README says 2.35. Conservative, therefore safe. Tightening it\nwould buy nothing and risk being wrong on a target we do not test.\n\n## Suggested wording\n\nName both floors and both reasons, since the C++ one has a cause worth stating: the binary links\nlibstdc++ because it embeds DuckDB. That also makes the RFC-0042 payoff legible - **removing DuckDB\nshould leave the binary needing libc, libm and libgcc only**, which is a better statement of the\nbenefit than purity."
  },
  {
    "number": 948,
    "title": "Slice 0's BOM covers Linux x86_64 only; aarch64-apple-darwin is unmeasured",
    "author": "cargopete",
    "state": "CLOSED",
    "url": "https://github.com/nightswatchhq/nuthatch/issues/948",
    "body": "Gap in RFC-0042 slice 0 (#935), stated there under \"what slice 0 does not answer\" and filed so it is\nnot lost.\n\nThe BOM was produced on **Linux x86_64**, the primary release target: 872 units, 223 s clean release\nbuild, and the attribution that qualified §1's premise (DuckDB 10.6% of build time against\nwasmtime+cranelift's 21.3%, while being 93% of native artefact bytes).\n\n**`aarch64-apple-darwin` is not measured.** It matters for two reasons that do not apply to Linux:\n\n- **The C++ evidence is linkage-shaped and macOS links differently.** On Linux the proof is\n  `libstdc++.so.6` in `ldd`. macOS uses `libc++` and `otool -L`; the equivalent statement has to be\n  re-established rather than assumed.\n- **Cross-compilation is one of §1's named costs.** A BOM from one platform cannot speak to it.\n\n`scripts/native-bom.sh` runs anywhere with a checkout and a toolchain, but its object-file scan and\n`stat`/`find -printf` usage are GNU-shaped and will need the BSD equivalents.\n\nNot blocking: slice 0's conclusions about roles and build-time share hold for the target that\nmatters most, and §13's gates can be met on Linux alone. This is completeness."
  },
  {
    "number": 241,
    "title": "Nest authoring DX: silent factory-rule collision, _dec columns that don't exist, and no subgraph import path",
    "author": "cargopete",
    "state": "CLOSED",
    "url": "https://github.com/nightswatchhq/nuthatch/issues/241",
    "body": "Feedback from authoring a nest by hand — porting a real, stuck subgraph (`QmVPhLwuv9zn2c761Ua7eJAFXBmrNsW32XUYsj1GKtNX4X`, the POA DAO stack on Arbitrum One) into [`nightswatchhq/poa-nest`](https://github.com/nightswatchhq/poa-nest). 19 contracts, 207 tables, a factory, 42.5M blocks.\n\nThe outcome was good — **848 events sealed in 59s** against a subgraph that had been stuck >24h. Everything below is friction met on the way, ordered by severity. Items 1 and 2 are correctness bugs; the rest are DX.\n\n---\n\n## 1. Duplicate factory rules silently collide — and the log reports a count that isn't true\n\n`FactorySet::build` keys rules in a `HashMap` by `{watch}__{event}` (`src/factory.rs:97`). Two factories on the same event overwrite each other. There is no duplicate check — the two `bail!`s above it only cover unknown `watch`/`template`.\n\n**Reproduced.** Two factories on one event, different `child_param`, different template:\n\n```toml\n[[factories]]\nwatch = \"org_registry\"\nevent = \"ContractRegistered\"\nchild_param = \"beacon\"\ntemplate = \"switchable_beacon\"\n\n[[factories]]\nwatch = \"org_registry\"\nevent = \"ContractRegistered\"\nchild_param = \"proxy\"\ntemplate = \"module_proxy\"\n```\n\nStartup logs:\n\n```\nfactory nest: 2 template(s), 2 rule(s) - topic0-only tip fetch, children discovered at runtime\n10 × factory discovered module_proxy child\n 0 × factory discovered switchable_beacon child\n```\n\nIt **claims 2 rules**, then runs one. `switchable_beacon` gets tables, gets a `__children` view, and stays empty forever. No warning, no error. A user reasonably reads \"2 rule(s)\" as confirmation.\n\nWorse, it silently depends on config ordering — the last declaration wins.\n\n**Why it matters beyond the log line:** this is the natural shape for a whole class of protocols. `OrgDeployer.OrgDeployed` announces **ten** module addresses in one event, one per named param:\n\n```solidity\nevent OrgDeployed(bytes32 indexed orgId, address indexed executor, address hybridVoting,\n                  address directDemocracyVoting, address quickJoin, address participationToken,\n                  address taskManager, address educationHub, address paymentManager,\n                  address eligibilityModule, address toggleModule, ...);\n```\n\nTen factory rules on that event is the obvious encoding, and it silently yields one. I had to pin all ten module addresses as static contracts instead — fine here because the deployment has exactly one org, but it doesn't generalise: a second org needs a config edit and a redeploy, which is precisely what factories exist to avoid.\n\n**Suggested fix**, in ascending order of usefulness:\n1. `bail!` on a duplicate `{watch}__{event}` — at minimum, never silently drop one.\n2. Make `rules` a `Vec` / `HashMap<String, Vec<FactoryRule>>` so one event can announce N children of N templates. `discover()` returns `Vec<ChildEntry>` instead of `Option`.\n3. Longer term: a `child_param` that accepts several params, or template selection keyed on a discriminator param (`typeId` here) — the other half of this pattern, where one event announces one child whose *type* is a field.\n\n---\n\n## 2. `_dec` columns vanish without `schema.json`, but `/schema` keeps advertising them\n\nThe `*_dec` companions are derived from `schema.json` (`src/analytics.rs:765,956`). The **footgun text is derived from the ABI** (`src/semantic.rs:388`). Those two sources disagree, and nothing checks.\n\nI hand-wrote `nuthatch.toml` rather than running `init`, so there was no `schema.json`. Result:\n\n```\n$ nuthatch sql \"SELECT count(*) FROM information_schema.columns WHERE column_name LIKE '%_dec'\"\n0\n$ curl -s localhost:8288/schema | grep -c \"_dec\"\n196\n```\n\n**Zero `_dec` columns existed; `/schema` recommended them on 196 lines.** Verbatim from the schema tool:\n\n```\n⚠ big-int columns (exact text; use the `_dec` companion for SUM/AVG/compare): delta → delta_dec, usedInEpoch → usedInEpoch_dec\n```\n\nNeither column exists. An agent doing exactly what the schema tool instructs gets `Binder Error: Referenced column \"delta_dec\" not found`. This is the AI-native surface confidently describing a schema that isn't there — the one place being wrong is most expensive.\n\n`nuthatch schema` fixes it instantly (137 `_dec` columns appeared). The problem is nothing told me it was needed.\n\n**Suggested fix:**\n- Warn at `dev` startup when `schema.json` is missing or older than `nuthatch.toml` — a one-liner pointing at `nuthatch schema` would have saved the whole detour. `src/sql_errors.rs:59` already has this hint; it just fires too late, only after a failed query.\n- Better: derive the footgun advice from the same source as the columns, so they cannot disagree.\n- Best: have `dev` regenerate derived artifacts when stale, the way it already rebuilds the child registry.\n\n---\n\n## 3. Views never load on a cold start\n\n`views/*.sql` load once at startup. On a fresh nest there is no data, so *every* view fails:\n\n```\nWARN view 10-org.sql failed to load: Catalog Error: Table with name org_deployer__org_deployed does not exist!\nWARN view 20-module.sql failed to load: ...\n```\n\nThe backfill then succeeds and the views are simply absent until you restart. So the documented first-run — \"`nuthatch dev`, then query\" — cannot use views on the first run, which is the run that forms someone's impression of the tool. My README has to say \"start it, Ctrl-C, start it again\", which is a shame for a project whose pitch is <2 minutes to first query.\n\n**Suggested fix:** re-attempt view loading after the initial backfill completes (and after the first seal), not only at startup.\n\n---\n\n## 4. One empty table kills an entire view file, one error at a time\n\nA table only exists in DuckDB once it has rows, so referencing a never-fired event is a catalog error that fails **the whole file** — including views that would have worked. The error names only the first missing table, so it's fix → restart → next error → repeat.\n\nThis bites hardest on exactly the events worth having a view for: `TaskCancelled`, and the direct-democracy voting module (deployed and configured, no proposals yet). Both are now commented out in the published nest with a note to uncomment when they fire, which is unsatisfying — those views are correct, just premature.\n\n**Suggested fix:** the full table set is known statically from the ABIs + config — `nuthatch schema` already computes all 207. Create empty typed views for tables with no rows (there's already machinery near `src/analytics.rs:1011,1620` doing this for the sealed/hot union). Failing that: skip the failing statement rather than the file, and report **all** unresolved tables in one message.\n\n---\n\n## 5. There is no subgraph import path — and this is the biggest single DX win available\n\nI did by hand what a command could do: fetch the manifest from IPFS, pull the 24 ABIs from its pinned CIDs, map `dataSources` → `[[contracts]]`, map templates + their creating events → `[[templates]]`/`[[factories]]`, carry `startBlock`, and derive aliases.\n\n```sh\nnuthatch init --from-subgraph QmVPhLwuv9zn2c761Ua7eJAFXBmrNsW32XUYsj1GKtNX4X\n```\n\nThe ABIs pinned by a manifest are strictly better than Sourcify for this codebase — every module is a beacon proxy, so Sourcify returns the proxy ABI with none of the real events. The manifest is the only good source, and it's already content-addressed.\n\nEven a 70%-correct scaffold plus an honest report would be transformative:\n\n```\n✓ 6 static dataSources → contracts\n✓ 24 ABIs vendored from pinned CIDs\n⚠ 10 file/ipfs dataSources skipped — nuthatch indexes metadata hashes, not CID content\n⚠ PoaManagerSatellite points at 0x0 — skipped\n⚠ 19 templates: 4 resolved to singletons, 1 factory rule inferred, 14 need a creating event\n```\n\n\"Point it at your stuck subgraph, get a working nest\" is a far stronger story than \"read the docs and write TOML\" — and the migration path *is* the product here.\n\n---\n\n## 6. No RPC preflight — I lost real time to endpoints that can't do the job\n\nA usable Arbitrum endpoint needs wide `eth_getLogs` **and** JSON-RPC batches >3 **and** archive depth. Measured:\n\n| Endpoint | Wide getLogs | Batch >3 | Archive |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| `arb1.arbitrum.io/rpc` | ✅ | ✅ (rate-limits into partial responses near tip) | ✅ |\n| `arbitrum-one.public.blastapi.io` | ❌ capped | ✅ | ✅ |\n| `1rpc.io/arb` | ❌ 50-block cap | ✅ | ✅ |\n| `arbitrum.drpc.org` | — | ❌ free plan caps at 3 | — |\n| `arb-pokt.nodies.app` | ❌ 403 | — | — |\n\nEach failure surfaced only at runtime, as a retry loop that looks like slowness rather than misconfiguration. `nuthatch doctor --rpc <url>` probing max getLogs width, max batch size and archive depth — and printing the largest safe `--window` — would turn 20 minutes of bisection into one command.\n\n---\n\n## 7. The timestamp-batch narrowing can't converge on a *count* limit (RFC-0029)\n\nAgainst `arbitrum.drpc.org` the narrowing walked `781 → 234 → 220 → 218 → 218 …` and stalled. The endpoint's limit is **batch count** (\"Batch of more than 3 requests are not allowed on free plan\"), not payload size, so shrinking the block window never helps — the batch is still >3.\n\n```\nWARN block timestamps unavailable for 489612751..=489613531: block_timestamps batch failed after\nretries: HTTP 500 ... \"Batch of more than 3 requests are not allowed on free plan\" - retrying window\n```\n\nIt retries forever without progressing. Worth classifying that error distinctly and either capping batch *size* or failing fast with \"this endpoint caps batches at N; it cannot serve timestamps\".\n\nRelated: partial responses are correctly refused (`66/244 block(s) missing ... refusing a partial map`) — good call, that would have sealed `block_timestamp=0`. But at the tip on a rate-limited endpoint it also retries indefinitely.\n\n---\n\n## 8. The skill's `cli-reference.md` is missing 6 commands, including the fix for #2\n\n`.claude/skills/nuthatch-builder/cli-reference.md` says it is `GENERATED by nuthatch skill-refs` and \"If a flag isn't here, it doesn't exist — never invent one\". Diffing it against `nuthatch --help`:\n\n```\nmissing: control, metadata, recipe, schema, serve, worker\n```\n\n`nuthatch schema` is the exact remedy for issue 2, and the one authoritative document says it doesn't exist. That's how I ended up debugging missing `_dec` columns from source. Regenerating in CI (there's already a check for `config-reference.md`) would keep it honest.\n\n---\n\n## 9. Smaller things\n\n- **`nuthatch check` has no `--url`.** `sql` has one; `check` requires stopping `dev` first. The error is helpful (`tip: to pass '--url' as a value, use '-- --url'`) but the asymmetry is surprising for the one command you want in CI against a live nest.\n- **`nuthatch sql` could auto-detect a running instance** on the default port instead of erroring about a locked store — the message is good, the extra step isn't needed.\n- **`--concurrency` is silently ignored for factory nests**: `factory backfill runs sequentially (--concurrency 8 ignored until the step-5 filter flip)`. Clear and honest, just worth noting it's a real throughput ceiling for factory nests today.\n\n---\n\n## What worked well, for balance\n\n- **Wide `--window` on a sparse contract set is the whole ballgame.** 42.5M blocks in 59s.\n- **Factory discovery is genuinely good** where one event maps to one child type — 10/10 beacons found, correct on restart, rebuilt from stored events (`rebuilt child registry: 10 discovered child contract(s)`).\n- **The footgun surface is the right idea** — reserved words, big-int guidance and overflow warnings are exactly what an agent needs. Issue 2 is a plumbing bug, not a design flaw.\n- **`checks/` + recorded fixtures** made parity verifiable rather than asserted, and caught nothing only because it was right.\n- **Refusing to seal partial timestamp maps** is precisely the correct instinct.\n\nHappy to send PRs for any of these — 1, 2 and 8 look small and high-value.\n"
  }
]
